# Unit DAG threshold 64 v1 launch review

**Verdict: HOLD pending a metadata correction.** No compiler or runtime command was run.

The plan, freeze, and launch hashes are `67ddd3cb909aec54b268d146b9aa7ce93e48179f009cc6b6b14f160315a1400e`, `d205e6f3e82f9a8a3ec2cdcfc7cad29c0f02e1bf099032a4ed1e7a9a046289b0`, and `5d0252daecead6ee7903eee5d6fd86562fbd3206d41aea8d0efeb5906f8cdbcc`. All 144 file pins and six resource-root inventories match. The plan retains all 53 Unit source files, the pinned 40,265,217-byte generated C input, and the 207,834-byte full consumer oracle. The new native binary, receipt, raw streams, and guard files are absent.

The build command changes only the DAG threshold from 128 to 64 (apart from the per-sequence native output path); CPU 3, `-O3`, Clang, input C, libraries, and the 120/5-second build/runtime caps remain bound. Pinned LLVM 19.1.7 source shows `HugeRegion` triggers map reduction and the default reduction size is half that threshold, so 64 uses 32; this remains an experimental scheduling/quality tradeoff, not proof or a performance claim.

The plan `scope` incorrectly still says “only experimental dag-maps-huge-region128 added,” although the command supplies 64. The existing collector copies this field into the receipt. Correct the current plan scope to 64 and freeze a successor before launch; no other blocker was found.
